  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: ClinPhys 4
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: 'During a Phase I clinical trial, you notice that one patient has developed
    a fever and rash after the second dose. Despite these side effects, the patient
    wants to continue because they feel hopeful about the treatment.


    <b>As
    a physician, what is the most appropriate action?</b>'
  explanation: "Physicians play a key role in protecting the safety and well-being
    of patients during a clinical trial. If a patient has an unexpected reaction
    during the trial, the physician should pause treatment and report what happened
    right away. This allows the research team to carefully evaluate whether the treatment
    is still safe and decide how best to support the patient\u2019s health as the
    study continues. It also helps researchers understand how and why certain side
    effects occur, which can improve safety monitoring for future patients."
  correctAnswer: "Pause the patient\u2019s treatment and report the side effects
    to the research team for evaluation"
  incorrectAnswer1: Allow the patient to continue the trial because they want to
  incorrectAnswer2: Increase the dose to test whether the reaction becomes worse
  incorrectAnswer3: Ignore the side effects because mild side effects are expected
    in Phase I trials
  questionRole: Physician
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Clinical
